#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124208 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124645 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "639785dd-cc89-52aa-84a2-84fc139c979d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Quadratic Function"}] :question/id #uuid "5c276425-f5ef-585f-9ab3-1b0262fff08e" :question/math-academy-id "e-12456" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y) = x^2+y^2\\,$ on the ellipse $x^2 +4y^2-4=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)= x^2 +4y^2 -4=0.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y), \\qquad g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,8y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2x=2\\lambda x \\\\ 2y=8\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1-\\lambda )=0 \\\\ y(4\\lambda -1)=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $x^2 +4y^2 -4  =0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{2}-4 & =0\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(0,1 \\right), \\left(0,-1 \\right).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =1$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(4(1)-1)=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition $x^2 +4y^2 -4  =0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-4 & =0\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(-2,0 \\right), \\left(2,0 \\right).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0, \\pm 1), \\qquad (\\pm 2,0).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,\\pm 1)$ | $(\\pm 2, 0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $4$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the points $(0,\\pm 1),$ and its minimum value is $1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124421 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124609 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eefd0f3a-69c8-5bd3-b062-8e0a896eed06" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Linear Function"}] :question/id #uuid "dc39fdca-dfbc-5806-b0aa-8480ee44b10f" :question/math-academy-id "e-8689" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y) = x+y\\,$ on the ellipse $x^2 + 2y^2 =6.$" :question/worked-solution "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)= x^2 + 2y^2 = 6.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y), \\qquad g(x,y)=6\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,4y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda = 0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda\\neq 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}1=2\\lambda x \\\\ 1=4\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{2\\lambda } \\\\ y=\\frac{1}{4\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the condition $g(x,y) = 6$ and solve for $\\lambda,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{1}{2\\lambda })}^{2}+2\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4\\lambda })}^{2} & =6 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{16{\\lambda }^{2}} & =6 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{8{\\lambda }^{2}} & =6 \\\\ \\frac{2}{8{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{8{\\lambda }^{2}} & =6 \\\\ \\frac{2+1}{8{\\lambda }^{2}} & =6 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x$ and $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx= \\pm 2 \\qquad y = \\pm 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n\\left(-2, -1 \\right), \\quad \\left(2, 1 \\right).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\, (x,y) \\,$ | $\\, \\left(-2, -1\\right) \\,$ | $\\, \\left(2, 1 \\right) \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, f(x,y) \\,$ | $\\, -3 \\,$ | $\\, 3 \\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at the point $\\left(2, 1 \\right),$ and its maximum value is $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124424 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124612 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5328ab3-440f-5cc8-9b8f-e7f86a4dd740" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Three-Variable Function"}] :question/id #uuid "ebdcad27-1ee2-57e7-9ebf-29ccf0a8ad12" :question/math-academy-id "e-8736" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y,z) = x+y+z$ on the ellipsoid $x^2 + y^2 + 4z^2 = 36.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the maximum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z) = x^2 + y^2 + 4z^2 = 36.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y,z), \\qquad g(x,y,z) = 36\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y,8z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda = 0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\neq 0,$ we have the following:\n![](images/c6/c6423b8d22015c99c9ec599c5417cdfd723d764d57d32f06ff49a28ec27c0c9e.svg)\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x,\\, y$ and $z$ into the condition $g(x,y,z) = 36$ and solve for $\\lambda,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2} & =36 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+4(\\frac{1}{64{\\lambda }^{2}}) & =36 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{16{\\lambda }^{2}} & =36 \\\\ \\frac{9}{16{\\lambda }^{2}} & =36 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{64} \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x,\\, y$ and $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx = \\pm 4 \\qquad y = \\pm 4 \\qquad z= \\pm 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-4, -4, -1),  \\qquad (4, 4, 1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\, (x,y,z) \\,$ | $\\, (-4, -4, -1) \\,$ | $\\, (4, 4, 1)  \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, f(x,y,z) \\,$ | $\\, -9 \\,$ | $\\, 9 \\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(4, 4, 1),$ and its maximum value is $9.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435021 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124612 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5328ab3-440f-5cc8-9b8f-e7f86a4dd740" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Three-Variable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74f6294a-88a3-5d3b-88cc-14fd7eb79196" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435013} {:answer/id #uuid "7ed5b1f0-05f9-560f-88c6-5e0131619d51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435014} {:answer/id #uuid "9cee8778-b90f-5b02-a141-07a6688cbe28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435015} {:answer/id #uuid "c8ae345c-fc6f-54f0-b244-c53690489f87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435017} {:answer/id #uuid "e857a965-5a56-5906-8d05-63cb1084b4ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435018}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ed5b1f0-05f9-560f-88c6-5e0131619d51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435014} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d6cce28-cf25-55c2-b5f4-befc5a6846de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435020}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "21c855c5-92b9-55cc-b57d-bfcc7ea11d89" :question/math-academy-id "q-101317" :question/problem "Find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=3y+3z$ on the ellipsoid ${x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}=48.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the maximum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}=48.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=48\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,3,3⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,4y,8z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0=2\\lambda x \\\\ 3=4\\lambda y \\\\ 3=8\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=\\frac{3}{4\\lambda } \\\\ z=\\frac{3}{8\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x,\\,y$ and $z$ into the condition $g(x,y,z)=48$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2} & =48 \\\\ {0}^{2}+2(\\frac{9}{16{\\lambda }^{2}})+4(\\frac{9}{64{\\lambda }^{2}}) & =48 \\\\ \\frac{9}{8{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{9}{16{\\lambda }^{2}} & =48 \\\\ \\frac{18}{16{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{9}{16{\\lambda }^{2}} & =48 \\\\ \\frac{27}{16{\\lambda }^{2}} & =48 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =\\frac{9}{256} \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $y$ and $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\ny=\\pm 4,\\,z=\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,-4,-2),\\,(0,4,2).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,(0,-4,-2)\\,$ | $\\,(0,4,2)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,-18\\,$ | $\\,18\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(0,4,2),$ and its maximum value is $18.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483364 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124609 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eefd0f3a-69c8-5bd3-b062-8e0a896eed06" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Linear Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "112e5b58-a689-5138-b33f-e47c56e7167c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482839} {:answer/id #uuid "40fc280e-d5f1-5a84-9cfc-9c3ad50813fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482920} {:answer/id #uuid "7228d70a-0771-5134-8636-ee3ba6b53f0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483011} {:answer/id #uuid "d9e6b23f-d37b-563e-840d-2a93839faacd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483179} {:answer/id #uuid "f0bafb54-6e90-57a9-943c-bbd0971097f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483215}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "112e5b58-a689-5138-b33f-e47c56e7167c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482839} :answer-field/id #uuid "70d3adf1-3aa8-506a-a4e2-98cf256eb5b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483284}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4126f1c5-7567-585d-9e49-d37c72e8e18c" :question/math-academy-id "q-162753" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=4x-2y+1\\,$ on the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5.$" :question/worked-solution "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=5\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨4,-2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}4=2\\lambda x \\\\ -2=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{2}{\\lambda } \\\\ y=-\\frac{1}{\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=5$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =5 \\\\ \\frac{4}{{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =5 \\\\ \\frac{5}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =5 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x$ and $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm 2,\\,y=∓1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-2,1),\\,(2,-1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $(-2,1)$ | $(2,-1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-9$ | $11$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-2,1),$ and its minimum value is $-9.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281534 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124645 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "639785dd-cc89-52aa-84a2-84fc139c979d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Quadratic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19903984-b264-5218-8eb4-1754817c4b34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278817} {:answer/id #uuid "29ea5848-6c0e-5b94-8503-a8f6ed6b60e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278963} {:answer/id #uuid "59b75fd2-66e7-5fad-a650-d9d3b809c0cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279412} {:answer/id #uuid "c53a97c7-c796-5ee5-8e31-7c4f839fe642" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280325} {:answer/id #uuid "c895d60c-9c51-5767-9758-83f7673ee2c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280347}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c895d60c-9c51-5767-9758-83f7673ee2c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280347} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a9ab8dd-d09c-55cd-89e8-8d3c2a2afe61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281020}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "46c43068-59bd-54f5-b56f-0c41ad44d87a" :question/math-academy-id "q-162760" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2\\,$ on the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8=0.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨8x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x=8\\lambda x \\\\ 2y=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x(1-4\\lambda )=0 \\\\ y(1-\\lambda )=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-8 & =0\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,-2\\sqrt{2}),(0,2\\sqrt{2}).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{4}$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1-\\frac{1}{4})=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\pm \\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(-\\sqrt{2},0),(\\sqrt{2},0).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0),\\,(0,\\pm 2\\sqrt{2}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)$ | $(0,\\pm 2\\sqrt{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $4$ | $10$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the points $(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)$ and its minimum value is $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281536 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124645 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "639785dd-cc89-52aa-84a2-84fc139c979d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Quadratic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10e57bce-4288-5fe3-aa0a-73628c52743d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278746} {:answer/id #uuid "5d3b843e-b72a-51a2-a995-da57e46e487b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279446} {:answer/id #uuid "81d5aaa0-fd9f-568f-9bb1-e082fcdaa7de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279754} {:answer/id #uuid "a999c752-b365-5d3b-960d-66d2a2fbec26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280097} {:answer/id #uuid "ced37650-ae67-5a4c-bc4a-bb74c5223c77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280414}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81d5aaa0-fd9f-568f-9bb1-e082fcdaa7de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279754} :answer-field/id #uuid "5be271dc-3c10-5641-8030-505366b398d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280990}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "76775e9c-a3b9-5240-b988-cfc2af339188" :question/math-academy-id "q-162764" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+1\\,$ on the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$" :question/worked-solution "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=4\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨4x,-2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}4x=2\\lambda x \\\\ -2y=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x(2-\\lambda )=0 \\\\ y(\\lambda +1)=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4 & =0\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,-2),(0,2).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =2$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(2+1)=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(2,0),(-2,0).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,\\pm 2),\\,(\\pm 2,0).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,\\pm 2)$ | $(\\pm 2,0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-3$ | $9$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the points $(0,\\pm 2)$ and its minimum value is $-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281540 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124645 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "639785dd-cc89-52aa-84a2-84fc139c979d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Quadratic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "067d5a2d-6869-56e6-bd30-d1d637da0ed9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278652} {:answer/id #uuid "3ab6b9aa-6893-5c30-bca8-5fdb9563537d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279112} {:answer/id #uuid "6a010beb-65c4-5e35-ad5c-7bf949fd509f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279550} {:answer/id #uuid "9f954b66-ca3e-5344-8471-57f7679c4f0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280009} {:answer/id #uuid "a5a7d1eb-7c25-5d15-88c4-89c4d05dde4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280060}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f954b66-ca3e-5344-8471-57f7679c4f0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280009} :answer-field/id #uuid "100f5724-313a-5fd9-a67e-3b7db596f576" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280860}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "65f180ab-0ef8-5dc1-be74-075a62b075ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-162769" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=2x-{y}^{2}\\,$ on the hyperbola ${y}^{2}-{x}^{2}=3.$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the global maximum of $f(x,y)$ exists on the hyperbola.*" :question/worked-solution "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}-3=0.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,-2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨-2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2=-2\\lambda x \\\\ -2y=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}1-x\\lambda =0 \\\\ y(\\lambda +1)=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition ${y}^{2}-{x}^{2}-3=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-3 & =0\\,⇒\\,{x}^{2}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives a contradiction.\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =-1$ in the second equation of the system, the first equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+x(-1)=0\\,⇒\\,x=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition ${y}^{2}-{x}^{2}-3=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4 & =0\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(-1,-2),(1,2).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(1,-2),\\,(1,2).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(1,-2)$ | $(1,2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-2$ | $-2$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at the points $(1,\\pm 2)$ and its maximum value is $-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281542 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124612 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5328ab3-440f-5cc8-9b8f-e7f86a4dd740" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Three-Variable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73b616d3-0939-5fd5-b676-b982b7500fb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279638} {:answer/id #uuid "b1257dbb-5373-5443-935b-b1acd74d8ac8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280160} {:answer/id #uuid "bac8313c-c838-5efc-904d-997a176d5fb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280239} {:answer/id #uuid "bcd32add-b28e-54a1-966e-d02d89ab3ca8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280252} {:answer/id #uuid "f4924c76-6566-5fd0-af93-99fdfdf8208c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280734}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1257dbb-5373-5443-935b-b1acd74d8ac8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280160} :answer-field/id #uuid "b47f103f-4fb3-53b4-9075-36e278e4c72a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281154}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7782a0c2-b648-5acd-8aa6-4c23463ce5b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-162773" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ on the ellipsoid $9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=36.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the minimum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z)=9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=36.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=36\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,2y,2z⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨18x,8y,2z⟩ \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨9x,4y,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=9\\lambda x \\\\ y=4\\lambda y \\\\ z=\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}(1-9\\lambda )x=0 \\\\ (1-4\\lambda )y=0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )z=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- First, assume that $\\lambda \\ne \\frac{1}{9},$ $\\lambda \\ne \\frac{1}{4}$ and $\\lambda \\ne 1.$ Then, the solution of the above system is $x=y=z=0.$ But this contradicts the constraint $g(x,y,z)=36.$\n\n- Next, assume that $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{9}.$ Then, by the second and third equations, we must have $y=z=0.$ Also, by the constraint $g(x,y,z)=36,$ we have\n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}=36\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm 2,0,0).$\n\n- Now, assume that $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{4}.$ Then, by the first and third equations, we must have $x=z=0.$ Also, by the constraint $g(x,y,z)=36,$ we have\n\n$$\n4{y}^{2}=36\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 3.\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,\\pm 3,0).$\n\n- Finally, assume that $\\lambda =1.$ Then, by the first and second equations, we must have $x=y=0.$ Also, by the constraint $g(x,y,z)=36,$ we have\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}=36\\,⇒\\,z=\\pm 6.\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,0,\\pm 6).$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,(\\pm 2,0,0)\\,$ | $\\,(0,\\pm 3,0)\\,$ | $\\,(0,0,\\pm 6)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,4\\,$ | $\\,9\\,$ | $\\,36\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the points $(\\pm 2,0,0),$ and its minimum value is $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483353 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124609 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eefd0f3a-69c8-5bd3-b062-8e0a896eed06" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Linear Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "077e60b6-8dfd-5425-af73-cfd611ef7918" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482822} {:answer/id #uuid "53ae6944-c0ae-5fb2-bd44-030a207fcefc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482958} {:answer/id #uuid "70f63dec-0347-57c6-8669-f185a64af767" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483009} {:answer/id #uuid "e12ede27-37da-589d-8cba-76441bf6394f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483192} {:answer/id #uuid "fde02425-ec57-5a63-be2e-568b6cad03db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5+\\sqrt{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70f63dec-0347-57c6-8669-f185a64af767" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483009} :answer-field/id #uuid "6db7cbdb-ccee-5e24-89d3-8d2ca2dc081d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483282}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "27a9cc77-ffaa-5079-b6d3-f595b1244e8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-162776" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=10x-2\\sqrt{15}y\\,$ on the ellipse $5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5.$" :question/worked-solution "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=5\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨10,-2\\sqrt{15}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨10x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}10=10\\lambda x \\\\ -2\\sqrt{15}=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{\\lambda } \\\\ y=-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=5$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{15}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =5 \\\\ \\frac{5+15}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =5 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =4 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x$ and $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,y=∓\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}),\\,(\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2})\\,$ | $\\,(\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2})\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,-20\\,$ | $\\,20\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}),$ and its minimum value is $-20.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483396 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124645 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "639785dd-cc89-52aa-84a2-84fc139c979d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Quadratic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c313004-6208-55c4-a29e-409a760d3bd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482914} {:answer/id #uuid "4fd23171-d788-5e6e-8ad6-c9430da94769" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482952} {:answer/id #uuid "711f158a-fa20-5db5-8546-777a45f7ce7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483010} {:answer/id #uuid "8ba5be8a-a984-5074-bd2c-9994fe1ca5be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483057} {:answer/id #uuid "d28aa3fe-8784-549c-be59-9299e7bbdf0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483166}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "711f158a-fa20-5db5-8546-777a45f7ce7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483010} :answer-field/id #uuid "110d77ff-11e1-54ff-9518-294a8feb70c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483246}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b0386d5f-8012-5e50-aca9-5585df04ede1" :question/math-academy-id "q-162779" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,$ on the parabola ${x}^{2}-2y-4=0.$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the global minimum of $f(x,y)$ exists on the parabola.*" :question/worked-solution "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}-2y-4=0.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2x=2\\lambda x \\\\ 2y=-2\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1-\\lambda )=0 \\\\ y=-\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}-2y-4=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2y-4 & =0\\,⇒\\,y=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $(0,-2).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =1$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}-2y-4=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2-4 & =0\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm \\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(-\\sqrt{2},-1),(\\sqrt{2},-1).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,-2),\\,(\\pm \\sqrt{2},-1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,-2)$ | $(\\pm \\sqrt{2},-1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $4$ | $3$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the points $(\\pm \\sqrt{2},-1)$ and its minimum value is $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281545 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124612 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5328ab3-440f-5cc8-9b8f-e7f86a4dd740" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Three-Variable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5638563b-d158-5175-b2f5-18a361140f17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279381} {:answer/id #uuid "7e887422-ae84-50f5-853b-33b1d408c13e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279729} {:answer/id #uuid "a77ef715-9d0b-5ab8-a99c-76a216a12600" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280075} {:answer/id #uuid "d1dd546b-fe71-5a90-abcc-def6f1c66b85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280445} {:answer/id #uuid "e035be03-2737-5cd2-8654-1d0a31906f99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280572}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a77ef715-9d0b-5ab8-a99c-76a216a12600" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280075} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4777f6b-d7c6-5b5e-bc28-b7c8021b9ea6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281266}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e0089be7-076e-5546-9485-8927c1e7080e" :question/math-academy-id "q-162784" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+3$ on the surface ${z}^{2}-xy-4=0.$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the global minimum of $f(x,y,z)$ exists on the surface.*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the minimum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z)={z}^{2}-xy-4=0.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,2y,2z⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨-y,-x,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x=-\\lambda y \\\\ 2y=-\\lambda x \\\\ 2z=2\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}(4-{\\lambda }^{2})x=0 \\\\ (4-{\\lambda }^{2})y=0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )z=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- First, assume that $\\lambda \\ne 1$ and $\\lambda \\ne \\pm 2.$ Then, the solution of the above system is $x=y=z=0.$ But this contradicts the constraint $g(x,y,z)=0.$\n\n- Next, assume that $\\lambda =-2.$ Then we must have $x=y$ and $z=0.$ But this also contradicts the constraint $g(x,y,z)=0.$\n\n- Now, assume that $\\lambda =1.$ Then, by the first and second equations, we must have $x=y=0.$ Also, by the constraint $g(x,y,z)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}-4=0\\,⇒\\,z=\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,0,\\pm 2).$\n\n- Finally, assume that $\\lambda =2.$ Then we must have $x=-y$ and $z=0.$ Also, by the constraint $g(x,y,z)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n-(-y)y-4=0\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(∓2,\\pm 2,0).$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,(0,0,\\pm 2)\\,$ | $\\,(∓2,\\pm 2,0)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,7\\,$ | $\\,11\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the points $(0,0,\\pm 2),$ and its minimum value is $7.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281549 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124609 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eefd0f3a-69c8-5bd3-b062-8e0a896eed06" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Linear Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f99e9c7-cb36-5228-9c41-28e4961f2d97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279009} {:answer/id #uuid "54d4e3a5-d812-5fba-b7ce-960cac114d20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279363} {:answer/id #uuid "9c9dc478-f346-5793-b1e8-9c5026604f5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279985} {:answer/id #uuid "9d2eb8b5-a992-5816-af7a-c95dadf4579b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279989} {:answer/id #uuid "f33bbf3a-c060-52f1-a2ed-041e9b5e1854" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280716}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f99e9c7-cb36-5228-9c41-28e4961f2d97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279009} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4166733-a580-5eab-a6a6-bfacef9ce080" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281231}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7651aa63-b4a3-56d9-b622-a30f9579f57e" :question/math-academy-id "q-162791" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=-4x+y+4\\,$ on the parabola $8x-{y}^{2}=1.$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the global maximum of $f(x,y)$ exists on the parabola.*" :question/worked-solution "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=8x-{y}^{2}=1.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-4,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨8,-2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-4=8\\lambda  \\\\ 1=-2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y=-\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now use $\\lambda $ to find $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{2(-\\frac{1}{2})}=1.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expression for $y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=1$ and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x-{y}^{2}=1 \\\\ 8x-1 & =1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has a possible extreme value at the point\n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{4},1).\n$$\n\nSince the given parabola opens to the right, there isn't a minimum value of $f$ on the parabola. Thus, the extreme value is the maximum of $f$ on the parabola. We evaluate $f$ at this point:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,(\\frac{1}{4},1)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,4\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(\\frac{1}{4},1),$ and its maximum value is $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362338 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124609 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eefd0f3a-69c8-5bd3-b062-8e0a896eed06" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Linear Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75b438b0-5bc9-5848-8c09-4b9737f5876d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361357} {:answer/id #uuid "a6441485-d04f-586c-8c4c-ff98f01eb432" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361581} {:answer/id #uuid "bb1f6919-eef4-5eeb-be4a-38877febdda3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361671} {:answer/id #uuid "e75d0f27-b8bb-5a5c-8905-034c15c6f3c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361870} {:answer/id #uuid "faf4cd0b-f3f9-5c75-a7ea-835686f7d019" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361959}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "faf4cd0b-f3f9-5c75-a7ea-835686f7d019" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361959} :answer-field/id #uuid "314fa88d-8f92-5297-95d3-76a7ddb9d25f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362031}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "47aa66c7-074f-539e-9248-f6b2a1659ce1" :question/math-academy-id "q-99030" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=3x-y+6\\,$ on the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=10.$" :question/worked-solution "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=10.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=10\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨3,-1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}3=2\\lambda x \\\\ -1=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{3}{2\\lambda } \\\\ y=-\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=10$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =10 \\\\ \\frac{10}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =10 \\\\ 4{\\lambda }^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x$ and $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm 3,\\,y=∓1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-3,1),\\,(3,-1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,(-3,1)\\,$ | $\\,(3,-1)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,-4\\,$ | $\\,16\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(3,-1),$ and its maximum value is $16.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362339 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124609 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eefd0f3a-69c8-5bd3-b062-8e0a896eed06" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Linear Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "075b3513-d2fc-5e9a-8ff0-2aab5db7cb27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360862} {:answer/id #uuid "0cbd7eda-9a45-5683-9e40-626dab02003b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360888} {:answer/id #uuid "b71306f7-0408-5b68-938c-45f1c168c526" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-40\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361654} {:answer/id #uuid "cff3bb4a-f1d0-5588-993c-52723759352e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361766} {:answer/id #uuid "dae90de0-58c1-535a-ae69-fa7602e42e25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0cbd7eda-9a45-5683-9e40-626dab02003b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360888} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e5dea45-a64a-5e4a-aa7f-b189e201e4ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362109}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "10fa34d1-bf77-58e6-9792-74938feb848e" :question/math-academy-id "q-99039" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=4x+16y\\,$ on the ellipse ${x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=4.$" :question/worked-solution "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=4.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=4\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨4,16⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,8y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}4=2\\lambda x \\\\ 16=8\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{2}{\\lambda } \\\\ y=\\frac{2}{\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=4$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{{\\lambda }^{2}}+4\\cdot \\frac{4}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{4+16}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =4 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =5 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x$ and $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}},\\,y=\\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}},-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}),\\,(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}},\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}},-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}})\\,$ | $\\,(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}},\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}})\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,-8\\sqrt{5}\\,$ | $\\,8\\sqrt{5}\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}},-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}),$ and its minimum value is $-8\\sqrt{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125263 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124645 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "639785dd-cc89-52aa-84a2-84fc139c979d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Quadratic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "808bc807-8985-5b9f-ab17-6129fae2d84e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124179} {:answer/id #uuid "af42351a-797f-5469-a433-81bbd3fc83af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124180} {:answer/id #uuid "40eb4d5e-0afd-5ebb-beae-fd412118155c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124181} {:answer/id #uuid "46dd9149-28f3-5511-8709-162fdd74dd30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124182} {:answer/id #uuid "5b77c6a0-38b1-5bf3-8a05-28b62f75fb21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124183}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2c2609f5-ed2a-56c5-8017-644ef95c6b63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124178} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32307806-5955-5592-95b0-6b2e3adfcb53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395577} {:answer/id #uuid "8add3fff-d190-501f-bb10-48b18eb21972" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395578} {:answer/id #uuid "55db6f99-f790-5443-95a3-e2e6e6ad6673" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395579} {:answer/id #uuid "d321d6af-443d-53c4-af2a-5534f866c431" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395580} {:answer/id #uuid "7335edf3-36b6-569c-82a9-31a630674e9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395581}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32307806-5955-5592-95b0-6b2e3adfcb53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395577} :answer-field/id #uuid "a37a1846-7305-5bee-939a-d49bb878e3e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395576}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4a77a271-f1dc-530a-b0b6-613b52cca69d" :question/math-academy-id "q-99040" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=4{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+5\\,$ on the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y=0.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨8x,4y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}8x=2\\lambda x \\\\ 4y=\\lambda (2y-2)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x(4-\\lambda )=0 \\\\ y(\\lambda -2)=\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-2y & =0\\,⇒\\,y=0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,0),(0,2).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =4$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(4-2)=4\\,⇒\\,y=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $(0,2).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(0,2).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(0,2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $5$ | $13$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at the point $(0,2)$ and its maximum value is $13.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362343 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124612 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5328ab3-440f-5cc8-9b8f-e7f86a4dd740" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Three-Variable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4507bd7c-efe5-5221-9de4-92390c22dbee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361136} {:answer/id #uuid "4e6d5810-052f-5906-b0f3-22a707c3f7ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361179} {:answer/id #uuid "62739a49-ca4b-5886-beda-164cff5d2454" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361278} {:answer/id #uuid "851c87f1-fe6b-5c46-9399-24694aa9ba06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361431} {:answer/id #uuid "ff306620-674d-545f-b05b-6b961df29ed1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361977}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4507bd7c-efe5-5221-9de4-92390c22dbee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361136} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab5ad21d-d745-57f6-82a3-1517eb6f070d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362139}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "50c63a8a-ed83-5245-b469-aa943d591180" :question/math-academy-id "q-99168" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}-yz$ on the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the maximum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-z,-y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x=2\\lambda x \\\\ -z=2\\lambda y \\\\ -y=2\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x(\\lambda -1)=0 \\\\ y(4{\\lambda }^{2}-1)=0 \\\\ z=-2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- First, assume that $\\lambda \\ne 1$ and $\\lambda \\ne \\pm \\frac{1}{2}.$ Then, the solution of the above system is $x=y=z=0.$ But this contradicts the constraint $g(x,y,z)=1.$\n\n- Next, assume that $\\lambda =1.$ Then, by the second and third equations, we must have $y=z=0.$ Also, by the constraint $g(x,y,z)=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=1\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 1.\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $(\\pm 1,0,0).$\n\n- Finally, assume that $\\lambda =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}.$ Then, by the first and third equations, we must have $x=0$ and $z=∓y.$ Also, by the constraint $g(x,y,z)=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+(-y)^{2}=1\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding values of $z$ are\n\n$$\nz=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}),(0,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}).$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,(0,-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})\\,$ | $\\,(0,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})\\,$ | $\\,(\\pm 1,0,0)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\,$ | $\\,∓\\frac{1}{2}\\,$ | $\\,1\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at the points $(\\pm 1,0,0),$  and its maximum value is $1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362344 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124612 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5328ab3-440f-5cc8-9b8f-e7f86a4dd740" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Three-Variable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e78c062-5bbb-5d47-8490-f632a903c3aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "66" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361393} {:answer/id #uuid "98b8f2f7-b57f-52bf-bba5-261c709ab958" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361516} {:answer/id #uuid "bd4f0a54-1da4-56c5-a251-31e76856ed42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361682} {:answer/id #uuid "c315158b-b316-5cbe-b0a2-4dbdc6349018" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361711} {:answer/id #uuid "cd96bea8-492f-5b32-ad46-f2b1b533ac30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361762}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd96bea8-492f-5b32-ad46-f2b1b533ac30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361762} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c5ea212-ecca-57c4-955b-c1894af98b79" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362074}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2d3ecce9-3af4-5e04-a08c-80960443b54f" :question/math-academy-id "q-99171" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=2x+6y+10z$ on the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=35.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the maximum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=35.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=35\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,6,10⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2=2\\lambda x \\\\ 6=2\\lambda y \\\\ 10=2\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{\\lambda } \\\\ y=\\frac{3}{\\lambda } \\\\ z=\\frac{5}{\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x,\\,y$ and $z$ into the condition $g(x,y,z)=35$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =35 \\\\ \\frac{1}{{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{9}{{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{25}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =35 \\\\ \\frac{35}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =35 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x,\\,y$ and $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm 1\\,y=\\pm 3\\,z=\\pm 5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-1,-3,-5),\\,(1,3,5).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,(-1,-3,-5)\\,$ | $\\,(1,3,5)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,-70\\,$ | $\\,70\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(1,3,5),$ and its maximum value is $70.$"}]}
